(RRB) benefit.","enroll_form.learn_more_payment_options.automatic_deduction.delay_warning":"It may take up to 3 months for your premiums to start being deducted. If you can't have your premiums deducted from your monthly benefit, your plan will notify you and send you a bill. You must pay the bill to keep your plan coverage.","enroll_form.learn_more_payment_options.change_how_you_pay":"If you want to change how you pay your premiums after your plan coverage starts, contact the plan.","enroll_form.learn_more_payment_options.pay_directly":"Pay your plan directly using one of your plan’s premium payment options. In addition to mailing your payment to the plan, they may offer automatic deduction from your checking or savings account, automatic credit card payments, online payment, or other ways. Contact your plan about ways you can pay your bill.","enroll_form.learn_more_payment_options.remember":"To keep your plan coverage, you must pay your plan premiums.","enroll_form.learn_more_payment_options.two_ways":"There are 2 ways you can pay your monthly plan premiums (including if you owe a late enrollment penalty):","enroll_form.long_payment_info":"<p>If you choose to have the monthly premium for this Medicare drug plan automatically deducted from your Social Security or RRB benefit. Please be advised that it may take up to two or more months to begin after Social Security or the RRB approve your request for automatic deduction. If Social Security or RRB does not approve your request for automatic deduction, the plan you have selected will send you a paper bill for your monthly premium. Also, if the plan you have selected sends your request for automatic deduction to Social Security or RRB later in the month, the automatic deduction will not start until after you have enrolled with your plan. If this happens, your plan will send you a paper bill for the premiums for the first month or two of your enrollment.</p><p>If it takes two months for deductions to begin, two monthly premiums will be withheld in a lump sum from your Social Security or RRB benefit.</p><p>If it takes three months for deductions to begin, three monthly premiums will be withheld in a lump sum from your Social Security or RRB benefit. You will never have a deduction that is more than $300 worth of premiums.</p><p>If you currently have Medicare drug plan premiums withheld from your Social Security or RRB benefit: Please be advised that it may take up to two or three months before deductions for your new plan begin. This means that you may have two or three monthly premiums from your old plan withheld from your Social Security or RRB benefit until the deductions begin. When the deductions begin for your new plan, there will be an adjustment for any premiums withheld from your old plan, as well as the deduction for your new plan.</p><p>If you have assistance other than extra help from SSA or your State Medicaid: When another organization, such as a State Pharmacy Assistance Program, pays a portion of your drug plan premium and you choose to have automatic premium withholding from your SSA or RRB benefits, the entire premium amount will be deducted. Your drug plan will then send you a refund for the overpayment of premiums. If you do not wish to have the entire premium deducted from your SSA or RRB benefits, you can choose to pay any balance for your premium directly to the plan (this will mean you would pay only your portion of the premium and the other organization would pay its portion).</p>","enroll_form.long_term_care_facility.name":"Name of facility","enroll_form.long_term_care_facility.name.error":"Enter the name of the facility.","enroll_form.long_term_care_question":"Are you a resident of a long-term care facility like a nursing home?","enroll_form.mailing_address":"Your mailing address","enroll_form.male":"Male","enroll_form.mapd_type.label":"Medicare Advantage with drug coverage","enroll_form.ma_type.label":"Medicare Advantage without drug coverage","enroll_f